    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, '1: During a patient interview, the physician uses phrases like "Uh-huh," "Go on," and "I see." Which interviewing technique is this?', '["Facilitation", "Reflection", "Clarification", "Validation"]', 0, 'Facilitation encourages the patient to continue talking without directing the topic of conversation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '2: A patient states, "I''ve been having terrible chest pain for a week." The physician replies, "You''ve been having chest pain for a week." This is an example of:', '["Validation", "Reflection", "Empathy", "Summarization"]', 1, 'Reflection involves repeating the patient''s own words back to them to encourage deeper exploration.'),
    (v_topic_id, '3: When assessing a patient''s "History of Present Illness" (HPI), what mnemonic is classically used to ensure all characteristics of the symptom are covered?', '["SOAP", "OPQRST", "SIGECAPS", "CAGE"]', 1, 'OPQRST stands for Onset, Provocation/Palliation, Quality, Region/Radiation, Severity, and Time.'),
    (v_topic_id, '4: The "CAGE" questionnaire is a validated screening tool used to detect which of the following?', '["Depression", "Domestic violence", "Alcohol abuse", "Cognitive impairment"]', 2, 'CAGE stands for Cut down, Annoyed, Guilty, and Eye-opener, used for screening alcohol use disorder.'),
    (v_topic_id, '5: When taking a sexual history, which approach is most appropriate to normalize the questioning and reduce patient anxiety?', '["Ask only if the patient brings it up", "Use a direct, closed-ended approach to get through it quickly", "Use an introductory normalizing statement like, ''Because sexual health is important to overall health, I ask all my patients...''", "Delegate this portion to a self-administered questionnaire only"]', 2, 'Normalizing statements help patients feel that their experience is common and that the physician asks these questions routinely.'),
    (v_topic_id, '6: A 65-year-old patient presents with weight loss. Which feature of the history would most strongly point toward an organic (medical) cause rather than a psychiatric cause?', '["Loss of appetite", "Increased appetite with weight loss", "Early morning awakening", "Anhedonia"]', 1, 'Weight loss despite an increased appetite suggests hyperthyroidism, malabsorption, or a hypermetabolic state, which are organic causes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '7: When evaluating a patient with a new symptom, what does the "A" in the mnemonic "OLD CARTS" stand for?', '["Aggravating/Alleviating factors", "Associated symptoms", "Acuity", "Area"]', 0, 'OLD CARTS stands for Onset, Location, Duration, Character, Aggravating/Alleviating factors, Radiation, Timing, and Severity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '8: During the physical exam, the physician percusses the patient''s abdomen and hears a high-pitched, musical sound. This sound is best described as:', '["Resonance", "Hyperresonance", "Tympany", "Dullness"]', 2, 'Tympany is the high-pitched, musical sound heard over air-filled structures like the stomach or intestines.'),
    (v_topic_id, '9: Which part of the stethoscope is best used to detect low-pitched sounds, such as the murmurs of mitral stenosis or S3?', '["The diaphragm applied firmly", "The diaphragm applied lightly", "The bell applied firmly", "The bell applied lightly"]', 3, 'The bell, applied lightly to the skin, is best for low-pitched sounds. Firm pressure turns the skin itself into a diaphragm, filtering out low pitches.'),
    (v_topic_id, '10: A blood pressure cuff that is too small (narrow) for a patient''s arm will typically result in a reading that is:', '["Falsely low", "Falsely high", "Accurate for systolic but low for diastolic", "Accurate for diastolic but low for systolic"]', 1, 'A cuff that is too narrow or short requires higher pressure to compress the artery, leading to a falsely elevated blood pressure reading.'),
    (v_topic_id, '11: What is the defining characteristic of pulsus paradoxus?', '["A drop in systolic blood pressure > 10 mmHg during inspiration", "An alternating strong and weak pulse", "A delayed pulse at the femoral artery compared to the radial artery", "A pulse that disappears upon palpation"]', 0, 'Pulsus paradoxus is an exaggeration of the normal drop in systolic blood pressure during inspiration, classically seen in cardiac tamponade and severe asthma.'),
    (v_topic_id, '12: A patient presents with a respiratory rate of 28 breaths per minute, characterized by deep, labored, and gasping breathing. This pattern is known as:', '["Cheyne-Stokes respiration", "Biot''s respiration", "Kussmaul breathing", "Agonal breathing"]', 2, 'Kussmaul breathing is a deep, rapid respiratory pattern often associated with severe metabolic acidosis, particularly diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).'),
    (v_topic_id, '13: What is the classic presentation of Cheyne-Stokes respiration?', '["Periods of deep breathing alternating with periods of apnea", "Irregular periods of apnea alternating with regular breathing", "Continuous deep, rapid breathing", "Prolonged inspiratory phase with short expiratory phase"]', 0, 'Cheyne-Stokes is a cyclical pattern of gradually increasing then decreasing tidal volumes followed by a period of apnea, often seen in heart failure or bilateral cortical lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, '14: When measuring the jugular venous pressure (JVP), the vertical distance is measured from the sternal angle. How many centimeters must be added to this measurement to estimate right atrial pressure?', '["2 cm", "5 cm", "8 cm", "10 cm"]', 1, 'The right atrium lies approximately 5 cm below the sternal angle regardless of the patient''s position.'),
    (v_topic_id, '15: A 25-year-old tall, thin male presents with sudden onset sharp chest pain and dyspnea. Physical exam reveals hyperresonance to percussion and decreased breath sounds on the right side. Diagnosis?', '["Pneumonia", "Pleural effusion", "Pneumothorax", "Asthma exacerbation"]', 2, 'Spontaneous pneumothorax presents with hyperresonance and decreased breath sounds due to air accumulation in the pleural space.'),
    (v_topic_id, '16: On lung examination, the physician notes dullness to percussion, increased tactile fremitus, and egophony over the left lower lobe. These findings indicate:', '["Pleural effusion", "Pneumothorax", "Lung consolidation (e.g., lobar pneumonia)", "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"]', 2, 'Consolidation transmits low-frequency sounds (tactile fremitus) better than air-filled lung and causes dullness to percussion and egophony (E-to-A change).'),
    (v_topic_id, '17: How do physical exam findings differ between a pleural effusion and lung consolidation?', '["Tactile fremitus is decreased in consolidation and increased in effusion", "Tactile fremitus is increased in consolidation and decreased in effusion", "Percussion is hyperresonant in effusion", "Breath sounds are vesicular over consolidation"]', 1, 'Fluid in the pleural space (effusion) insulates sound, decreasing tactile fremitus. Consolidation inside the lung enhances sound transmission to the chest wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, '18: A patient has a "barrel chest" and uses accessory muscles to breathe. Percussion of the chest is expected to reveal:', '["Dullness", "Resonance", "Hyperresonance", "Tympany"]', 2, 'A barrel chest and accessory muscle use suggest COPD (emphysema), which causes air trapping and hyperinflation, leading to hyperresonant percussion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '19: The presence of fine, end-inspiratory crackles (rales) at the lung bases is most characteristic of which condition?', '["Asthma", "Early congestive heart failure", "Pneumothorax", "Acute bronchitis"]', 1, 'Fine crackles at the bases indicate fluid shifting in the alveoli or opening of collapsed small airways, classic for pulmonary edema in early CHF.'),
    (v_topic_id, '20: What finding is considered a hallmark of a severe asthma exacerbation on physical exam?', '["A loud expiratory wheeze", "A silent chest (absence of wheezing)", "Egophony", "Increased tactile fremitus"]', 1, 'A "silent chest" indicates airflow is so severely obstructed that no sound is generated; it represents an impending respiratory arrest.'),
    (v_topic_id, '21: When auscultating the heart, the S1 sound is produced primarily by the closure of which valves?', '["Aortic and Pulmonic", "Mitral and Tricuspid", "Aortic and Mitral", "Mitral only"]', 1, 'S1 ("lub") is caused by the closure of the atrioventricular (mitral and tricuspid) valves at the onset of ventricular systole.'),
    (v_topic_id, '22: The physiological splitting of the S2 heart sound occurs during which phase of the respiratory cycle?', '["Inspiration", "Expiration", "Both inspiration and expiration equally", "During a Valsalva maneuver"]', 0, 'Inspiration decreases intrathoracic pressure, increasing venous return to the right heart. This delays pulmonic valve closure, causing A2 to occur before P2.'),
    (v_topic_id, '23: Fixed splitting of the S2 heart sound (split during both inspiration and expiration) is the hallmark of which congenital heart defect?', '["Ventricular septal defect", "Atrial septal defect", "Patent ductus arteriosus", "Tetralogy of Fallot"]', 1, 'An ASD causes continuous equalization of blood volumes between the right and left atria, delaying pulmonic closure uniformly regardless of the respiratory cycle.'),
    (v_topic_id, '24: A late-peaking systolic murmur that radiates to the carotid arteries is heard best at the right second intercostal space. Diagnosis?', '["Mitral regurgitation", "Aortic stenosis", "Mitral valve prolapse", "Aortic regurgitation"]', 1, 'Aortic stenosis typically presents as a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids.'),
    (v_topic_id, '25: A holosystolic, blowing murmur heard best at the apex and radiating to the left axilla is characteristic of:', '["Aortic stenosis", "Tricuspid regurgitation", "Mitral regurgitation", "Mitral stenosis"]', 2, 'Mitral regurgitation presents as a holosystolic murmur at the apex. Radiation to the axilla is classic.'),
    (v_topic_id, '26: Which maneuver increases the intensity of the murmur of Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM)?', '["Squatting", "Passive leg raise", "Valsalva maneuver", "Handgrip"]', 2, 'Valsalva decreases venous return (preload), which decreases left ventricular volume and worsens the outflow tract obstruction in HCM, making the murmur louder.'),
    (v_topic_id, '27: A patient has an early diastolic, decrescendo, blowing murmur heard best along the left sternal border. They also have "water-hammer" pulses. Diagnosis?', '["Aortic regurgitation", "Mitral stenosis", "Aortic stenosis", "Pulmonic regurgitation"]', 0, 'Aortic regurgitation presents with an early diastolic murmur and a widened pulse pressure, leading to bounding (water-hammer) peripheral pulses.'),
    (v_topic_id, '28: What is the classic auscultatory finding of mitral stenosis?', '["A mid-systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur", "A low-pitched, rumbling diastolic murmur with an opening snap", "A holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla", "An early diastolic decrescendo murmur"]', 1, 'Mitral stenosis is characterized by an opening snap (when the stiff valve opens) followed by a low-pitched diastolic rumble heard best at the apex with the bell.'),
    (v_topic_id, '29: The S3 heart sound ("ventricular gallop") is heard during early diastole. In adults over 40, it is highly specific for:', '["Aortic stenosis", "Congestive heart failure (volume overload)", "Pericarditis", "Mitral valve prolapse"]', 1, 'An S3 occurs during rapid ventricular filling into a non-compliant or overfilled left ventricle, classically indicating heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.'),
    (v_topic_id, '30: A patient presents with pleuritic chest pain that improves when sitting up and leaning forward. Auscultation reveals a scratchy, high-pitched, triphasic sound. Diagnosis?', '["Acute myocardial infarction", "Aortic dissection", "Acute pericarditis", "Pulmonary embolism"]', 2, 'The pericardial friction rub is the hallmark physical exam finding of acute pericarditis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '31: During the abdominal exam, what is the correct sequence of assessment techniques?', '["Inspection, Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation", "Inspection, Auscultation, Percussion, Palpation", "Auscultation, Inspection, Palpation, Percussion", "Palpation, Percussion, Auscultation, Inspection"]', 1, 'Auscultation must follow inspection but precede percussion and palpation to avoid altering normal bowel sounds.'),
    (v_topic_id, '32: A patient presents with right upper quadrant abdominal pain. Palpation of the RUQ during deep inspiration causes the patient to suddenly stop breathing. This is called:', '["Rovsing sign", "Psoas sign", "Murphy sign", "McBurney sign"]', 2, 'Murphy''s sign (inspiratory arrest upon palpation of the gallbladder) is highly sensitive for acute cholecystitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '33: Pain in the right lower quadrant when deep palpation is applied to the left lower quadrant is known as:', '["Rovsing sign", "Obturator sign", "Psoas sign", "Kehr sign"]', 0, 'Rovsing''s sign indicates localized peritoneal irritation in the RLQ, classic for acute appendicitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '34: A patient with severe abdominal pain has rigid, board-like abdominal muscles that do not relax even when the patient exhales. This "involuntary guarding" suggests:', '["Gastroenteritis", "Peritonitis", "Irritable bowel syndrome", "Uncomplicated cholelithiasis"]', 1, 'Rigidity or involuntary guarding is a reflex spasm of the abdominal muscles in response to diffuse peritoneal inflammation (peritonitis).'),
    (v_topic_id, '35: During percussion of the liver span in the midclavicular line, what is the normal adult measurement?', '["2 to 4 cm", "6 to 12 cm", "14 to 18 cm", "18 to 22 cm"]', 1, 'The normal liver span in the midclavicular line is 6-12 cm. Greater spans suggest hepatomegaly.'),
    (v_topic_id, '36: A patient with cirrhosis presents with ascites. When percussing the abdomen with the patient supine, what pattern of sounds is expected?', '["Tympany in the center, dullness in the flanks", "Dullness in the center, tympany in the flanks", "Tympany throughout", "Dullness throughout"]', 0, 'Air-filled bowels float to the top (center) causing tympany, while ascitic fluid pools in the dependent flanks, causing dullness.'),
    (v_topic_id, '37: To confirm the presence of ascites, the examiner taps one flank sharply and feels for an impulse on the opposite flank. This test is called:', '["Shifting dullness", "Fluid wave test", "Puddle sign", "Ballottement"]', 1, 'The fluid wave test detects free fluid in the peritoneal cavity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '38: A pulsatile mass is palpated in the midline of the abdomen just superior to the umbilicus. This finding most strongly suggests:', '["Pancreatic pseudocyst", "Abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Gastric carcinoma", "Distended bladder"]', 1, 'An expanding, pulsatile midline mass is the classic physical finding of an Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA).'),
    (v_topic_id, '39: A patient presents with acute, severe tearing pain radiating to the back. There is a blood pressure discrepancy of 30 mmHg between the right and left arms. Diagnosis?', '["Acute myocardial infarction", "Aortic dissection", "Acute pancreatitis", "Pulmonary embolism"]', 1, 'A tearing back pain with a blood pressure differential between arms is the classic presentation for an acute aortic dissection.'),
    (v_topic_id, '40: Costovertebral angle (CVA) tenderness is elicited by striking the back below the 12th rib. Positive CVA tenderness is a hallmark of:', '["Cholecystitis", "Pyelonephritis", "Appendicitis", "Pancreatitis"]', 1, 'CVA tenderness indicates inflammation of the kidney capsule, typically seen in pyelonephritis or renal colic.'),
    (v_topic_id, '41: A patient has drooping of the right upper eyelid (ptosis), a constricted right pupil (miosis), and lack of sweating on the right face (anhidrosis). This triad defines:', '["Horner syndrome", "Oculomotor nerve (CN III) palsy", "Bell''s palsy", "Myasthenia gravis"]', 0, 'Horner syndrome results from a lesion in the sympathetic pathway supplying the head and neck.'),
    (v_topic_id, '42: A complete left oculomotor nerve (CN III) palsy typically presents with:', '["Ptosis, miosis, and an eye looking \"down and out\"", "Ptosis, mydriasis (dilated pupil), and an eye looking \"down and out\"", "No ptosis, miosis, and an eye looking straight ahead", "No ptosis, mydriasis, and an eye looking medially"]', 1, 'CN III controls the levator palpebrae (ptosis) and parasympathetics (loss causes mydriasis). The unopposed CN IV and CN VI pull the eye "down and out."'),
    (v_topic_id, '43: When testing visual fields by confrontation, a patient cannot see fingers wiggling in the right temporal field and the left temporal field. This defect is called:', '["Homonymous hemianopsia", "Bitemporal hemianopsia", "Quadrantanopia", "Central scotoma"]', 1, 'Bitemporal hemianopsia indicates a lesion compressing the optic chiasm, most classically a pituitary adenoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, '44: Upon fundoscopic examination, the physician observes blurring of the optic disc margins, loss of the optic cup, and engorged veins. This finding is termed:', '["Macular degeneration", "Papilledema", "Glaucomatous cupping", "Diabetic retinopathy"]', 1, 'Papilledema is swelling of the optic disc secondary to increased intracranial pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '45: A patient has sensorineural hearing loss in the right ear. During the Weber test, where will the tuning fork sound lateralize?', '["To the right (affected) ear", "To the left (unaffected) ear", "Equally to both ears", "It will not be heard at all"]', 1, 'In sensorineural hearing loss, the Weber test lateralizes to the healthy (unaffected) ear because the damaged cochlea/nerve on the affected side cannot process the bone conduction.'),
    (v_topic_id, '46: In a patient with conductive hearing loss in the right ear (e.g., due to cerumen impaction), what will the Rinne test show on the right?', '["Air conduction > Bone conduction (AC > BC)", "Bone conduction > Air conduction (BC > AC)", "Air conduction = Bone conduction", "No sound heard by either pathway"]', 1, 'In conductive loss, bone conduction bypasses the middle ear blockage and is heard better than air conduction (BC > AC, which is an abnormal Rinne test).'),
    (v_topic_id, '47: A patient sticks out their tongue, and it deviates to the right. This indicates a lesion of which cranial nerve?', '["Right hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "Left hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)", "Right glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "Left vagus nerve (CN X)"]', 0, 'The genioglossus muscle pushes the tongue forward. A lesion of CN XII causes weakness on that side, so the intact opposite muscle pushes the tongue toward the weak (lesioned) side.'),
    (v_topic_id, '48: When the patient says "Ah," the uvula deviates to the left side. This indicates a lesion of the:', '["Right glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "Left glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "Right vagus nerve (CN X)", "Left vagus nerve (CN X)"]', 2, 'The vagus nerve elevates the palate. A right CN X lesion causes the right palate to droop, and the intact left CN X pulls the uvula toward the normal (left) side.'),
    (v_topic_id, '49: During the physical exam, brushing a cotton wisp on the cornea fails to elicit a blink in the left eye, but the right eye blinks. Assuming intact facial nerves, the lesion is in the:', '["Left ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve (V1)", "Right ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve (V1)", "Left facial nerve (CN VII)", "Right facial nerve (CN VII)"]', 0, 'The corneal reflex afferent is V1 (feels the cotton); the efferent is CN VII (closes both eyes). Failure of both eyes to blink when the left cornea is touched indicates a left V1 sensory deficit.'),
    (v_topic_id, '50: A patient presents with sudden weakness of the entire right side of the face, including the forehead. They cannot close their right eye or wrinkle their right forehead. Diagnosis?', '["Left hemisphere stroke", "Right hemisphere stroke", "Right Bell''s palsy (Peripheral CN VII lesion)", "Left Bell''s palsy"]', 2, 'A peripheral CN VII lesion (Bell''s palsy) affects the entire half of the face. An upper motor neuron lesion (stroke) spares the upper forehead due to bilateral cortical innervation of the upper face.'),
    (v_topic_id, '51: Muscle strength is graded on a scale of 0 to 5. What does a grade of 3/5 indicate?', '["No muscular contraction", "Active movement against gravity but not against resistance", "Active movement against gravity and some resistance", "Normal strength"]', 1, 'Grade 3/5 means the muscle can overcome gravity but yields to any applied resistance.'),
    (v_topic_id, '52: Deep tendon reflexes are graded on a scale of 0 to 4+. What does a 4+ reflex indicate?', '["Normal", "Brisk, but normal", "Very brisk, hyperactive, with clonus", "Absent"]', 2, 'A 4+ reflex is hyperactive and accompanied by clonus, strongly indicating an upper motor neuron lesion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '53: Eliciting the biceps reflex tests which primary spinal nerve roots?', '["C5, C6", "C7, C8", "L3, L4", "S1, S2"]', 0, 'The biceps and brachioradialis reflexes test C5-C6. The triceps reflex tests C7-C8.'),
    (v_topic_id, '54: The patellar (knee jerk) reflex tests which primary spinal nerve roots?', '["C5, C6", "L1, L2", "L3, L4", "S1, S2"]', 2, 'The patellar reflex tests the L3 and L4 nerve roots.'),
    (v_topic_id, '55: A positive Babinski sign (upgoing great toe and fanning of other toes upon plantar stimulation) in an adult indicates:', '["A lower motor neuron lesion", "An upper motor neuron lesion (corticospinal tract damage)", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Normal physiology"]', 1, 'The Babinski sign is a classic indicator of upper motor neuron damage in anyone older than 1-2 years.'),
    (v_topic_id, '56: A patient stands with feet together and eyes closed. They immediately lose their balance and sway significantly. This is a positive:', '["Pronator drift test", "Romberg test", "Dix-Hallpike maneuver", "Lasegue test"]', 1, 'A positive Romberg test indicates a defect in proprioception (dorsal columns) or vestibular function, as vision has been removed as a compensatory mechanism.'),
    (v_topic_id, '57: When asking a patient to hold both arms forward with palms up and eyes closed, one arm begins to slowly pronate and drift downward. This is a positive:', '["Romberg test", "Pronator drift", "Asterixis", "Dysdiadochokinesia"]', 1, 'Pronator drift is a sensitive test for subtle upper motor neuron weakness in the contralateral hemisphere.'),
    (v_topic_id, '58: A patient is asked to rapidly tap their hand on their thigh, alternating palm up and palm down. They perform this in a clumsy, irregular manner. This finding is called:', '["Dysmetria", "Dysdiadochokinesia", "Asterixis", "Chorea"]', 1, 'Dysdiadochokinesia is the inability to perform rapid alternating movements, indicating a cerebellar lesion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '59: When tracing a patient''s finger as they point from their nose to the examiner''s finger, the patient''s finger wavers and misses the target. This is known as:', '["Intention tremor / Dysmetria", "Resting tremor", "Postural tremor", "Myoclonus"]', 0, 'Dysmetria (past-pointing) and intention tremors are classic signs of cerebellar dysfunction.'),
    (v_topic_id, '60: Which type of tremor is classically present at rest, resembles "pill-rolling," and improves with voluntary movement?', '["Essential tremor", "Intention tremor", "Parkinsonian tremor", "Physiologic tremor"]', 2, 'The classic Parkinsonian tremor occurs at rest (4-6 Hz) and typically dampens or disappears during deliberate action.'),
    (v_topic_id, '61: A patient presents with a stooped posture, short shuffling steps, and reduced arm swing. This gait is characteristic of:', '["Cerebellar ataxia", "Sensory ataxia", "Parkinsonism", "Spastic hemiparesis"]', 2, 'A shuffling, festinating gait is a hallmark of Parkinson''s disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, '62: A patient walks with a wide-based, staggering, unsteady gait. They cannot walk heel-to-toe (tandem walking). This gait is most likely due to:', '["Cerebellar dysfunction", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Parkinson''s disease", "Common peroneal nerve palsy"]', 0, 'A wide-based, ataxic gait that impairs tandem walking points heavily to a cerebellar lesion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '63: A patient lifts their leg high and slaps their foot down with each step to avoid dragging their toes. This "steppage gait" is associated with a lesion of the:', '["Femoral nerve", "Common peroneal nerve", "Obturator nerve", "Tibial nerve"]', 1, 'A steppage gait is caused by foot drop, which is due to weakness of the dorsiflexors innervated by the deep peroneal nerve (a branch of the common peroneal).'),
    (v_topic_id, '64: A patient presents with neck stiffness. The examiner flexes the patient''s neck, which causes involuntary flexion of the patient''s hips and knees. This is a positive:', '["Kernig sign", "Brudzinski sign", "Babinski sign", "Hoffmann sign"]', 1, 'Brudzinski''s sign is a sign of meningeal irritation (meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage).'),
    (v_topic_id, '65: The examiner flexes the patient''s hip and knee to 90 degrees, then attempts to extend the knee. This causes severe pain and resistance. This is a positive:', '["Kernig sign", "Brudzinski sign", "Lachman test", "McMurray test"]', 0, 'Kernig''s sign indicates meningeal irritation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '66: A patient with severe liver disease is asked to hold their arms out with wrists extended. The hands exhibit a brief, sudden, flapping tremor. This is known as:', '["Chorea", "Athetosis", "Asterixis", "Myoclonus"]', 2, 'Asterixis (liver flap) is a sign of hepatic encephalopathy or other metabolic encephalopathies.'),
    (v_topic_id, '67: When examining a breast lump, which characteristics are most suspicious for malignancy?', '["Soft, mobile, tender, well-demarcated", "Firm, mobile, rubbery, well-demarcated", "Hard, painless, irregular borders, fixed to surrounding tissue", "Fluctuant, tender, warm to touch"]', 2, 'Malignant breast masses are typically hard, fixed, poorly circumscribed, and painless. Skin dimpling or nipple retraction may also be present.'),
    (v_topic_id, '68: A "peau d''orange" (orange peel) appearance of the breast skin is caused by:', '["A benign fibroadenoma stretching the skin", "Lymphatic obstruction by a tumor", "An acute bacterial infection (mastitis)", "Normal aging changes"]', 1, 'Peau d''orange is highly suspicious for inflammatory breast cancer causing blockage of the subdermal lymphatics.'),
    (v_topic_id, '69: The straight leg raise test is performed to evaluate for:', '["Meningitis", "Lumbosacral radiculopathy (sciatica)", "Hip osteoarthritis", "Deep vein thrombosis"]', 1, 'Pain radiating down the leg past the knee when the straight leg is raised between 30 and 70 degrees suggests sciatic nerve root compression (L4-S1).'),
    (v_topic_id, '70: A patient reports knee pain. The examiner grasps the heel, flexes the knee, and applies valgus stress while internally rotating the tibia, then extends the knee. A palpable "click" is felt. This positive McMurray test indicates:', '["Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear", "Medial collateral ligament (MCL) tear", "Meniscal tear", "Patellofemoral syndrome"]', 2, 'The McMurray test is used to evaluate for tears in the medial or lateral meniscus of the knee.'),
    (v_topic_id, '71: The Lachman test is the most sensitive physical exam maneuver for diagnosing a tear of the:', '["Medial meniscus", "Lateral collateral ligament (LCL)", "Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL)", "Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)"]', 3, 'The Lachman test involves translating the tibia anteriorly with the knee flexed at 20-30 degrees; a soft endpoint indicates an ACL tear.'),
    (v_topic_id, '72: A positive anterior drawer test of the ankle (pulling the heel forward while stabilizing the tibia) indicates injury to which ligament?', '["Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)", "Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL)", "Deltoid ligament", "Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)"]', 0, 'The ATFL is the most commonly injured ligament in an inversion ankle sprain, and it prevents anterior subluxation of the talus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '73: A patient has shoulder pain. They are instructed to abduct their arm to 90 degrees and slowly lower it. The arm drops suddenly. This "drop arm test" indicates:', '["Bicipital tendinitis", "Rotator cuff tear (specifically supraspinatus)", "Adhesive capsulitis", "Acromioclavicular joint separation"]', 1, 'A complete tear of the supraspinatus tendon prevents the patient from smoothly controlling the lowering of the abducted arm.'),
    (v_topic_id, '74: The "empty can" test (Jobe''s test) specifically isolates and tests the strength of which rotator cuff muscle?', '["Infraspinatus", "Teres minor", "Subscapularis", "Supraspinatus"]', 3, 'The patient elevates the arms in the scapular plane with thumbs pointed down (like emptying a can); weakness indicates supraspinatus pathology.'),
    (v_topic_id, '75: Phalen''s test and Tinel''s sign at the wrist are used to diagnose:', '["De Quervain tenosynovitis", "Carpal tunnel syndrome", "Cubital tunnel syndrome", "Rheumatoid arthritis"]', 1, 'These tests evaluate for median nerve compression at the wrist (Carpal Tunnel Syndrome).'),
    (v_topic_id, '76: Pain reproduced by grasping the thumb inside a closed fist and ulnar-deviating the wrist (Finkelstein''s test) is diagnostic for:', '["Carpal tunnel syndrome", "De Quervain tenosynovitis", "Scaphoid fracture", "Trigger finger"]', 1, 'De Quervain tenosynovitis involves inflammation of the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons in the first dorsal compartment.'),
    (v_topic_id, '77: Palpating the "snuffbox" at the base of the thumb elicits severe point tenderness. This indicates a high likelihood of:', '["Colles fracture", "Scaphoid fracture", "Metacarpal fracture", "De Quervain tenosynovitis"]', 1, 'Snuffbox tenderness is the hallmark of a scaphoid (navicular) bone fracture, which is at high risk for avascular necrosis if missed.'),
    (v_topic_id, '78: A patient presents with a swollen, warm, erythematous knee. To differentiate between a joint effusion and superficial soft tissue swelling, the examiner should look for:', '["A positive bulge sign or ballottement of the patella", "A positive McMurray test", "A positive Lachman test", "Crepitus on range of motion"]', 0, 'The bulge sign and patellar ballottement specifically test for the presence of excess fluid within the knee joint capsule (effusion).'),
    (v_topic_id, '79: During a skin exam, a lesion is described as a macule. By definition, a macule is:', '["Raised and less than 1 cm", "Raised and greater than 1 cm", "Flat and less than 1 cm", "Fluid-filled and less than 1 cm"]', 2, 'A macule is a flat, non-palpable change in skin color that is less than 1 cm in diameter (e.g., a freckle).'),
    (v_topic_id, '80: A solid, raised skin lesion that is larger than 1 cm in diameter is properly termed a:', '["Papule", "Plaque", "Vesicle", "Macule"]', 1, 'A plaque is a palpable, solid elevation greater than 1 cm, often formed by the confluence of papules (e.g., psoriasis).'),
    (v_topic_id, '81: A fluid-filled blister that is larger than 1 cm in diameter is called a:', '["Vesicle", "Bulla", "Pustule", "Wheal"]', 1, 'A bulla is a large fluid-filled blister (> 1 cm), whereas a vesicle is small (< 1 cm).'),
    (v_topic_id, '82: The "ABCDE" rule is used to screen for melanoma. What does the "E" stand for?', '["Erythema", "Elevation", "Evolution", "Exudate"]', 2, 'Evolution refers to any change in the lesion''s size, shape, color, or new symptoms like bleeding or itching, which is highly suspicious for melanoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, '83: A patient has a hyperpigmented, velvety plaque on the back of their neck. This finding (Acanthosis Nigricans) is most commonly associated with:', '["Addison''s disease", "Insulin resistance (Type 2 Diabetes)", "Celiac disease", "Hypothyroidism"]', 1, 'Acanthosis nigricans is a cutaneous marker of hyperinsulinemia and insulin resistance.'),
    (v_topic_id, '84: In assessing mental status, asking a patient to count backward from 100 by 7s (Serial 7s) primarily tests:', '["Orientation", "Remote memory", "Attention and calculation", "Executive function"]', 2, 'Serial 7s (or spelling "WORLD" backward) tests a patient''s concentration, attention span, and working memory.'),
    (v_topic_id, '85: A patient is asked what a pen and a watch are. They correctly identify them. This tests for:', '["Apraxia", "Agnosia", "Aphasia", "Anomia (Naming)"]', 3, 'Naming objects is a standard part of assessing language function to detect anomia, a common finding in various aphasias and dementia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '86: A patient speaks fluently with a normal rhythm, but their sentences consist of nonsensical words ("word salad"), and they cannot comprehend the examiner''s instructions. This describes:', '["Broca''s aphasia", "Wernicke''s aphasia", "Global aphasia", "Conduction aphasia"]', 1, 'Wernicke''s (receptive) aphasia is characterized by fluent but meaningless speech and poor language comprehension.'),
    (v_topic_id, '87: A patient understands commands perfectly but struggles to articulate words. Their speech is halting, sparse, and effortful. This describes:', '["Broca''s aphasia", "Wernicke''s aphasia", "Global aphasia", "Conduction aphasia"]', 0, 'Broca''s (expressive) aphasia involves broken, non-fluent speech with relatively preserved comprehension, typically due to a frontal lobe lesion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '88: In the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), asking the patient to recall three words (e.g., Apple, Table, Penny) after 5 minutes tests:', '["Registration", "Short-term (Recent) memory", "Long-term (Remote) memory", "Language"]', 1, 'Recalling objects after a short delay specifically tests recent/short-term memory, which is impaired early in Alzheimer''s disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, '89: To examine the thyroid gland properly, the examiner should stand behind the patient, place fingers just below the cricoid cartilage, and ask the patient to:', '["Cough", "Swallow", "Take a deep breath", "Extend the neck fully"]', 1, 'Swallowing causes the thyroid gland and any associated nodules to move upward with the trachea, making them easier to palpate.'),
    (v_topic_id, '90: A hard, painless, enlarged supraclavicular lymph node on the left side (Virchow''s node) is highly suspicious for:', '["Streptococcal pharyngitis", "Tuberculosis", "Metastatic abdominal or thoracic malignancy", "Hodgkin''s lymphoma confined to the neck"]', 2, 'The left supraclavicular node drains the thoracic duct; an enlarged node here is a classic sign of an occult gastrointestinal or lung malignancy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '91: When examining the pulses in a 1-year-old child, simultaneous palpation of the radial and femoral pulses reveals that the femoral pulse is delayed and weaker. This suggests:', '["Normal physiology for age", "Tetralogy of Fallot", "Coarctation of the aorta", "Patent ductus arteriosus"]', 2, 'A radio-femoral delay with weak lower extremity pulses is the hallmark physical finding of coarctation of the aorta.'),
    (v_topic_id, '92: The Ortolani and Barlow maneuvers are performed on neonates to detect:', '["Congenital heart disease", "Developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)", "Clubfoot", "Spina bifida"]', 1, 'The Barlow maneuver attempts to dislocate the hip backwards, and the Ortolani maneuver attempts to reduce it back into the acetabulum, testing for DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, '93: What is a normal Babinski reflex in a 6-month-old infant?', '["The toes plantarflex (curl downward)", "The great toe dorsiflexes and the other toes fan out", "There is no response", "The foot withdraws rapidly"]', 1, 'An upgoing Babinski response is a normal, physiological finding in infants up to 12-24 months of age due to incomplete myelination of the corticospinal tracts.'),
    (v_topic_id, '94: Which lymph nodes, if palpable and enlarged in a healthy adult, are generally considered normal (non-pathological)?', '["Supraclavicular nodes", "Epitrochlear nodes", "Small (<1 cm), mobile inguinal and submandibular nodes", "Hard, fixed cervical nodes"]', 2, 'Small, rubbery, mobile "shotty" nodes in the submandibular or inguinal regions are common and usually represent past minor infections.'),
    (v_topic_id, '95: A patient has clubbing of the fingers. This physical exam finding is classically associated with which condition?', '["Iron deficiency anemia", "Chronic hypoxia (e.g., severe COPD or pulmonary fibrosis) or lung cancer", "Rheumatoid arthritis", "Hypothyroidism"]', 1, 'Clubbing (loss of the normal angle between the nail and nail bed) is a sign of chronic hypoxemia, cyanotic heart disease, or underlying lung malignancy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '96: When examining the eyes, the examiner shines a light into the right eye. The left pupil constricts. This is known as the:', '["Direct pupillary response", "Consensual pupillary response", "Accommodation reflex", "Corneal reflex"]', 1, 'The consensual response occurs when the contralateral pupil constricts in response to light shown in the ipsilateral eye.'),
    (v_topic_id, '97: A patient has an Argyll Robertson pupil. What is the classic finding?', '["The pupil is dilated and poorly reactive to light", "The pupil accommodates (constricts to near objects) but does not react to light", "The pupil is constricted and irregular but reacts normally to light", "The pupil oscillates rhythmically"]', 1, 'Argyll Robertson pupils ("prostitute''s pupils") accommodate but do not react to light, classically seen in neurosyphilis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '98: A "swinging flashlight test" is performed. When the light is swung from the normal left eye to the abnormal right eye, the right pupil appears to dilate. This finding is a:', '["Relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil)", "Horner syndrome", "Adie tonic pupil", "Normal response"]', 0, 'A Marcus Gunn pupil indicates disease of the optic nerve or retina on that side. The brain perceives less light when the flashlight is moved to the sick eye, causing both pupils to dilate relative to their state when light was on the healthy eye.'),
    (v_topic_id, '99: Upon otoscopic examination of a healthy ear, the "cone of light" reflection on the tympanic membrane is normally seen in which quadrant?', '["Anterosuperior", "Anteroinferior", "Posterosuperior", "Posteroinferior"]', 1, 'The cone of light radiates anteriorly and inferiorly from the umbo (center) of the tympanic membrane.'),
    (v_topic_id, '100: A patient complains of severe pain when the examiner pulls on the auricle (pinna) or pushes on the tragus. This finding strongly suggests:', '["Otitis media", "Otitis externa", "Mastoiditis", "Cerumen impaction"]', 1, 'Pain with manipulation of the external ear (tragal tenderness) is the hallmark of otitis externa ("swimmer''s ear"), not otitis media (middle ear infection).');
